e": "The district's R+8 lean, Wied's incumbency and roughly 14-to-1 fundraising advantage, plus a unanimous Safe/Likely R expert consensus and market pricing near 9% for the Democrat, all point the same direction. The forecast lands at a double-digit Republican margin with modest uncertainty given the absence of local polling.", "watch": "Any late independent polling showing Crosson within single digits, or a surge of Democratic outside spending, would warrant moving this back toward Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tony Wied (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Wisconsin's 8th District is a Republican-leaning northeastern seat where first-term incumbent Tony Wied faces Democrat Rick Crosson, a little-known challenger with minimal fundraising. Wied ran unopposed in his primary and has raised over $1 million, while Crosson has raised about $75,000 with no outside spending on either side.\n\nEvery major rating outlet places the race in the Safe or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets give the Democrat roughly a one-in-ten chance. Local coverage has focused on issue contrasts rather than a competitive race dynamic. The evidence points to a comfortable Wied re-election, with the main uncertainty being the lack of district-level polling rather than any sign of a genuine contest.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0582, "margin": -10.2}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.052, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.066, "margin": -11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0699, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.033, "margin": -12.0}]}